// Project settings for Rust editing in Zed.
//
// Two problems this solves, both caused by rust-analyzer running `cargo check`
// against the same `target/` directory that your own `cargo build`/`cargo
// run` (or the coding agent) uses:
//
//   1. By default rust-analyzer runs `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` on
//      every save. This workspace pulls in Bevy, so each save re-checks a large
//      dependency tree and pegs the CPU.
//   2. Cargo takes an exclusive lock on the target directory for the duration of
//      a build. While rust-analyzer's check holds it, the build you actually
//      asked for stalls waiting for the lock (and vice versa).
//
// `checkOnSave: false` stops the automatic on-save check entirely. To get cargo
// diagnostics on demand, run the "RL: cargo check" task (cmd-shift-p ->
// "task: spawn"), whose output is clickable.
//
// `cargo.targetDir: true` points rust-analyzer at its own subdirectory,
// `target/rust-analyzer`, so the cargo invocations it still performs on
// project load (metadata, build scripts, proc macros) can never lock the
// `target/` directory your builds use.
//
// See https://zed.dev/docs/languages/rust and
// https://rust-analyzer.github.io/book/configuration.html
{
  "lsp": {
    "rust-analyzer": {
      "initialization_options": {
        "checkOnSave": false,
        "cargo": {
          "targetDir": true,
        },
      },
    },
  },
}
